---
/**
 * The sky band under the hero, where the design puts an illustrated
 * Philadelphia skyline.
 *
 * The design rendered this as a flat-shaded WebGL city over a static image. The
 * 3D was dropped deliberately: three.js cost 504 KB — three quarters of all
 * JavaScript on the site — and ~2.5s of main-thread blocking on the
 * highest-traffic page, for decoration. The band keeps its place in the layout
 * and its sky colour, and the illustration returns as a repeating background.
 *
 * `skyline.webp` is cut from the PhilaCon Valley poster illustration, which is
 * portrait art: its rooflines span 2060px of a 3900px width, roughly 1.6:1,
 * where this band is closer to 5.5:1. No crop of the poster both fills the band
 * and keeps the rooflines in frame. So the tile is the poster's building cluster
 * with the sky knocked out, joined to a mirrored copy of itself — mirroring
 * makes the tile's left and right edges identical, so it repeats with no seam at
 * any width. It is rolled half a cluster sideways so the mirror axis lands
 * mid-block rather than on the matching pair of towers, where the symmetry
 * would read.
 *
 * `background-size: auto 100%` is what earns the tiling: the buildings scale to
 * whatever height the band is and repeat sideways to fill the width, so
 * rooflines stay in frame and bases stay flush at every viewport. A plain <img>
 * would have to commit to one aspect ratio and be cropped or shrunk away from it
 * everywhere else — at 390px wide the whole skyline would collapse to a 65px
 * sliver above the fold.
 */
---

<div
  data-pcv-skyline
  class="relative h-[180px] lg:h-[260px] bg-brand-sky overflow-hidden bg-[url('/images/skyline.webp')] bg-bottom bg-repeat-x bg-[length:auto_100%]"
  role="img"
  aria-label="An illustrated Philadelphia skyline in the PhilaCon Valley brand colours"
>
</div>
